{"content_hash": "9d844a8fe620b48c", "version_id": 519, "article_id": 52, "level_code": "ja_n2", "lang": "ja", "level": 4, "level_label": "N2 中高級", "title": "利率操纵で有罪となった元ドイツ銀行トレーダー、控訴審で逆転無罪", "title_zh": "因操纵利率被判刑的德意志银行前交易员，上诉后罪名被推翻", "has_translation": true, "topic": "business", "source": "bbc_business", "source_url": "https://www.bbc.co.uk/news/articles/cqgkvj10k6lno?at_medium=RSS&at_campaign=rss", "image": {"type": "photo", "url": "https://images.unsplash.com/photo-1620266757065-5814239881fd?ixid=M3wxMDk3Mzk1fDB8MXxzZWFyY2h8Mnx8ZGV1dHNjaGUlMjBiYW5rJTIwdHJhZGVyfGVufDB8MHx8fDE3OTE2Nzc5NDl8MA&ixlib=rb-4.1.0&w=1600&q=75&fm=jpg&fit=max", "provider": "unsplash", "credit": "Photo by Kanchanara on Unsplash", "author": "Kanchanara", "credit_url": "https://unsplash.com/@kanchanara?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "source": "Unsplash", "source_url": "https://unsplash.com/photos/person-using-black-laptop-computer-5hcV51EeeWc?utm_source=daily-english-news&utm_medium=referral", "fallback": {"type": "gradient", "from": "hsl(271, 42%, 62%)", "to": "hsl(309, 46%, 48%)", "label": "BUSINESS", "seed": "14bf80248d84"}}, "published_date": "2026-10-09", "word_count": 251, "reading_minutes": 0.8, "lead": "金融危機後の金利操作スキャンダルで有罪となった元銀行員の一人が、長い法廷闘争の末に無罪となった。英国の控訴院は、元ドイツ銀行トレーダーのクリスチャン・ビッター被告の有罪判決を破棄した。", "lead_zh": "金融危机后利率操纵丑闻中被判有罪的又一名前银行家，经过漫长的法律斗争后获判无罪。英国上诉法院撤销了德意志银行前交易员克里斯蒂安·比特的定罪判决。", "preview": "ビッター被告は2018年、指標金利ユーリボルを「操作」したとして懲役刑を受けた。同被告はスイスの自宅からビデオ回線で裁判を傍聴し、BBCに対し「この日のために非常に長い時間を待った」と語った。今週初めには、同じ英国の裁判所が元バークレイズ銀行員5人の有罪判決も破棄している。", "preview_zh": "比特于2018年因“操纵”基准利率欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）而被判入狱。他在瑞士通过视频连线旁听庭审，并对BBC表示：“我为这一天等了非常非常久。”本周早些时候，同一家英国法院还推翻了五名巴克莱银行前员工的定罪。", "has_audio": true, "audio": {"id": 507, "url": "https://pub-aba43a6fb1db4dc08fede1dbc81f3241.r2.dev/audio/52/ja_n2.mp3", "duration": 56.562, "size_bytes": 343152, "engine": "edge-tts", "voice": "ja-JP-NanamiNeural", "word_count": 168, "has_timeline": true}, "detail_url": "https://cdn.jsdelivr.net/gh/chjam12301-sys/news-etl@fc48a26/content/data/versions/519.json", "paragraphs": ["ビッター被告は2018年、指標金利ユーリボルを「操作」したとして懲役刑を受けた。同被告はスイスの自宅からビデオ回線で裁判を傍聴し、BBCに対し「この日のために非常に長い時間を待った」と語った。今週初めには、同じ英国の裁判所が元バークレイズ銀行員5人の有罪判決も破棄している。", "金融危機は2008年に始まり、世界に大きな経済的衝撃を与え、多くの国で景気後退を引き起こした。銀行家に対する世論の反発が高まる一方、金融部門は納税者の資金による救済で守られた。2012年には、危機発生時に銀行が貸出金利の設定過程で自らの立場を偽っていたことが発覚し、リボル事件が起きた。"], "paragraphs_zh": ["比特于2018年因“操纵”基准利率欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）而被判入狱。他在瑞士通过视频连线旁听庭审，并对BBC表示：“我为这一天等了非常非常久。”本周早些时候，同一家英国法院还推翻了五名巴克莱银行前员工的定罪。", "金融危机始于2008年，给全球带来巨大经济冲击，并在许多国家引发衰退。公众对银行家的愤怒不断升温，而金融部门却靠纳税人的资金救助得到保护。2012年，人们发现银行在危机爆发时于设定贷款利率的过程中歪曲自身状况，由此爆发了伦敦银行间同业拆借利率（Libor）丑闻。"], "body": "ビッター被告は2018年、指標金利ユーリボルを「操作」したとして懲役刑を受けた。同被告はスイスの自宅からビデオ回線で裁判を傍聴し、BBCに対し「この日のために非常に長い時間を待った」と語った。今週初めには、同じ英国の裁判所が元バークレイズ銀行員5人の有罪判決も破棄している。\n\n金融危機は2008年に始まり、世界に大きな経済的衝撃を与え、多くの国で景気後退を引き起こした。銀行家に対する世論の反発が高まる一方、金融部門は納税者の資金による救済で守られた。2012年には、危機発生時に銀行が貸出金利の設定過程で自らの立場を偽っていたことが発覚し、リボル事件が起きた。", "body_zh": "比特于2018年因“操纵”基准利率欧元银行间同业拆借利率（Euribor）而被判入狱。他在瑞士通过视频连线旁听庭审，并对BBC表示：“我为这一天等了非常非常久。”本周早些时候，同一家英国法院还推翻了五名巴克莱银行前员工的定罪。\n\n金融危机始于2008年，给全球带来巨大经济冲击，并在许多国家引发衰退。公众对银行家的愤怒不断升温，而金融部门却靠纳税人的资金救助得到保护。2012年，人们发现银行在危机爆发时于设定贷款利率的过程中歪曲自身状况，由此爆发了伦敦银行间同业拆借利率（Libor）丑闻。", "text": "ビッター被告は2018年、指標金利ユーリボルを「操作」したとして懲役刑を受けた。同被告はスイスの自宅からビデオ回線で裁判を傍聴し、BBCに対し「この日のために非常に長い時間を待った」と語った。今週初めには、同じ英国の裁判所が元バークレイズ銀行員5人の有罪判決も破棄している。 金融危機は2008年に始まり、世界に大きな経済的衝撃を与え、多くの国で景気後退を引き起こした。銀行家に対する世論の反発が高まる一方、金融部門は納税者の資金による救済で守られた。2012年には、危機発生時に銀行が貸出金利の設定過程で自らの立場を偽っていたことが発覚し、リボル事件が起きた。", "vocab": [{"word": "有罪判決", "pos": "noun", "zh": "有罪判决", "note": "法律新闻常用词，与「無罪判決」相对", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "破棄する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "撤销、推翻", "note": "用于法院撤销原判，如「判決を破棄する」", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "控訴院", "pos": "noun", "zh": "上诉法院", "note": "英国的上诉审法院", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "指標金利", "pos": "noun", "zh": "基准利率", "note": "金融报道核心词汇", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "操作", "pos": "noun", "zh": "操纵", "note": "此处指人为操控利率", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "懲役刑", "pos": "noun", "zh": "有期徒刑", "note": "法律用语", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "傍聴する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "旁听", "note": "指在法庭上听取审理", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "景気後退", "pos": "noun", "zh": "经济衰退", "note": "经济新闻常用词", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "世論", "pos": "noun", "zh": "舆论、民意", "note": "常与「反発」搭配", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "救済", "pos": "noun", "zh": "救助、纾困", "note": "此处指政府出资救助金融机构", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "発覚する", "pos": "verb", "zh": "被发现、曝光", "note": "用于丑闻或秘密被揭露", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "上訴", "pos": "noun", "zh": "上诉", "note": "比「控訴」范围更广", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "不正義", "pos": "noun", "zh": "不公正、不义", "note": "当事人表达不满时常用", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}, {"word": "隠す", "pos": "verb", "zh": "隐瞒、隐藏", "note": "此处指证据被压下", "phonetic": "", "en": ""}], "title_original": "Ex-Deutsche Bank trader jailed for rigging rates has conviction overturned", "summary_original": "The Court of Appeal quashes the conviction of Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader jailed in 2018.", "body_original": "Ex-Deutsche Bank trader jailed for rigging rates has conviction overturned\nBy\nAndy Verity\nFinancial investigations correspondent\nPublished\n9 October 2026\nAnother former banker sentenced in one of the biggest scandals of the financial crisis has had his conviction overturned following a long-running legal battle.\nThe Court of Appeal has quashed the conviction of Christian Bittar, a former Deutsche Bank trader who went to prison in 2018 for \"manipulating\" the benchmark interest rate, Euribor.\nIt comes after the same UK court overturned five convictions of ex-Barclays bankers in the rate rigging trials earlier this week.\nBittar, who watched proceedings via video link from Switzerland because he was not granted a visa to attend court, told the BBC: \"I have waited a very, very long time for this day.\"\nThe financial crisis began in 2008, sending huge economic shockwaves across the world and triggering recessions in many countries.\nThere was a public backlash against bankers, held by many to be responsible for the crisis, while the financial sector was protected by taxpayer-funded bailouts.\nThe Libor scandal erupted in 2012, when it was discovered that at the outbreak of the financial crisis, banks had been misrepresenting their positions during the process of setting the lending rate, helping to boost profits and mask difficulties.\nSome 19 City traders were convicted in the US and UK between 2015 and 2019 across nine criminal trials held in London and New York.\nEighteen of these have now been acquitted.\nOnly one former trader in the world now remains convicted: former Barclays trader Peter Johnson, who was also one of the original whistleblowers in the interest rate rigging scandal. He pleaded guilty on advice that he had little chance at winning at trial. The court confirmed that he too has now applied to appeal against his conviction.\nThe news has already prompted lawyers and senior politicians to press for the Bank of England and the Treasury to release all their records about their own roles in interest rate 'rigging' during the financial crisis, amid concerns raised of a cover-up of the role of central banks and governments.\n'Finally our injustice has been recognised'\nSpeaking after his conviction was quashed, Bittar said: \"Finally the injustice of what I and others suffered has been recognised.\n\"I am so grateful for those who stood by me through this ordeal and those who worked so tirelessly to correct it.\"\nHis wife, Caroline, who lives in the UK, said their family had lost 15 \"valuable years\", with their children \"growing up with this injustice\".\nShe added they were looking forward to \"enjoying life with our family and friends without this shadow hanging over us\".\nOn Wednesday, Jay Merchant, Jonathan Mathew, Alex Pabon, Colin Bermingham and Philippe Moryoussef saw their convictions for 'manipulating' the interest rate benchmarks Libor and Euribor quashed after a 10-year struggle for justice.\nThey launched fresh appeals against their jail sentences following a Supreme Court ruling last year that acquitted Tom Hayes, the first to be jailed for 'rigging' interest rates in 2015, and Carlo Palombo, jailed in 2019.\nTom Hayes, who was initially sentenced to 14 years, reduced on appeal to 11, is now seeking damages from his former employer UBS.\nThe traders were among 37 City traders and brokers prosecuted for \"manipulating\" the interest rate benchmarks Libor and Euribor, which track the cost of borrowing cash between the banks and have been used to set the interest rates on millions of mortgages and commercial loans.\nThe BBC has uncovered evidence of a much larger, state-led 'rigging' of interest rates, under pressure from central banks and governments across the world. Evidence implicating Downing Street and the Bank of England was suppressed throughout the criminal trials.\nFormer Conservative cabinet minister David Davis told the BBC the innocent traders had been the victims of a series of miscarriages of justice.\nHe said the people exonerated this week and before were \"the victims of a scapegoating exercise which arose as a result of the government's own misbehaviour in lowballing [falsely understating] Libor interest rates themselves, in order to try and rescue the economy after their own self-induced crash in the late 2000s\".\nIn 2015, during the first trial for interest rate 'rigging', of former UBS trader Hayes, Mr Justice Jeremy Cooke decided as a matter of law that any attempt to influence the rates could not be lawful, and that any rate influenced by commercial interest must automatically be false. Judges in the subsequent eight trials followed suit.\nHowever, in July 2025, the Supreme Court decided that all the judges had erred, misdirecting juries that it was wrong as a matter of law. Instead, whether the requests were right or wrong was properly a matter of fact for the jury to decide, not the judge.\nAll convictions in the United States were overturned in 2022 after an appeal court ruled that there was insufficient evidence that the traders' requests had broken any rules or laws.\nRelated topics\nUK Supreme Court\nDeutsche Bank\nBarclays\nSerious Fraud Office\nBanking\nMore on this story\nEx-bankers jailed for rigging rates have convictions quashed\nPublished\n3 days ago\nRate 'rigging' traders say they were scapegoated - now the Supreme Court will decide\nPublished\n26 May 2025", "fetched_at": "2026-10-11T00:19:08.412879", "timeline": [{"i": 0, "w": "ビッター", "s": 0.125, "e": 0.562, "sm": 125, "em": 562, "cs": 0, "ce": 4, "si": 0}, {"i": 1, "w": "被告", "s": 0.575, "e": 0.975, "sm": 575, "em": 975, "cs": 4, "ce": 6, "si": 0}, {"i": 2, "w": "は", "s": 0.988, "e": 1.125, "sm": 987, "em": 1125, "cs": 6, "ce": 7, "si": 0}, {"i": 3, "w": "2018", "s": 1.15, "e": 2.12, "sm": 1150, "em": 2120, "cs": 7, "ce": 11, "si": 0}, {"i": 4, "w": "年", "s": 2.12, "e": 2.362, "sm": 2120, "em": 2362, "cs": 11, "ce": 12, "si": 0}, {"i": 5, "w": "、", "s": 2.362, "e": 2.688, "sm": 2362, "em": 2687, "cs": 12, "ce": 13, "si": 0}, {"i": 6, "w": "指標", "s": 2.688, "e": 3.125, "sm": 2687, "em": 3125, "cs": 13, "ce": 15, "si": 0}, {"i": 7, "w": "金利", "s": 3.138, "e": 3.538, "sm": 3137, "em": 3537, "cs": 15, "ce": 17, "si": 0}, {"i": 8, "w": "ユーリボル", "s": 3.55, "e": 4.062, "sm": 3550, "em": 4062, "cs": 17, "ce": 22, "si": 0}, {"i": 9, "w": "を", "s": 4.075, "e": 4.237, "sm": 4075, "em": 4237, "cs": 22, "ce": 23, "si": 0}, {"i": 10, "w": "「", "s": 4.237, "e": 4.362, "sm": 4237, "em": 4362, "cs": 23, "ce": 24, "si": 0}, {"i": 11, "w": "操作", "s": 4.362, "e": 4.925, "sm": 4362, "em": 4925, "cs": 24, "ce": 26, "si": 0}, {"i": 12, "w": "」", "s": 4.925, "e": 5.112, "sm": 4925, "em": 5112, "cs": 26, "ce": 27, "si": 0}, {"i": 13, "w": "し", "s": 5.112, "e": 5.275, "sm": 5112, "em": 5274, "cs": 27, "ce": 28, "si": 0}, {"i": 14, "w": "た", "s": 5.287, "e": 5.387, "sm": 5287, "em": 5387, "cs": 28, "ce": 29, "si"